 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Projecting midpoints onto the sphere requires normalization. Floating-point errors in normalization can accumulate over multiple levels of recursion. It is advisable to re-normalize vertices periodically or use high-precision arithmetic for deep refinements [G].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** $k=0$ yields the base 60 triangles and 32 vertices. The formula holds for $k \ge 0$ [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary:** The method assumes a unit sphere centered at the origin [G].
+- **Non-Congruence on Sphere:** Unlike planar subdivision, the new triangles formed on the sphere are not congruent to each other or to the original triangles. This is due to the curvature of the sphere [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+- **Distortion:** While the starting triangles are more nearly planar than those from regular polyhedra (like the octahedron), some distortion still occurs during subdivision. However, this distortion is significantly less than in octahedron-based methods, which retain 90° angles and cause uneven distribution [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+- **Vertex Uniqueness:** When calculating midpoints, ensure that vertices shared by adjacent triangles are treated as the same point to maintain the integrity of the mesh structure.
 
